@MinorityOfOne75 @the_unitron @stern_man @Mill226 Well, maybe you should decide to be careful, analyze, and not rush to judge?

Franken was blindsided AND expected to form perfect response. "Now!"

And no, sorry, but in that moment, with the HEAT of #MeToo, all responses were tightrope walks. Even w/time. Which he wasn't given.

@CarrieSweet2017 @JCrongeyer @alfranken I'd almost like to believe Dems were that naive (not that it would be complimentary in supposed elected leaders) but I don't think that was it.

I think was about their own profiles. They wanted to be seen as pure wrt #MeToo.

A bunch have regrets, too. Some almost immediately.

@MsTessMcGill @the_unitron @JakeLobin Mayer, on allegation #6:

"The [Boston Globe] has standards requiring #MeToo accusers to be identified or to corroborate their story through documents and witnesses—preferably, people outside their immediate circle. The Globe deemed the story too weak."

(Would apply to #7, too!)

@mostestghostess Weird then that HE was the one pleading for the investigation to proceed?

And weird that when one of best investigative journalists, #metoo cred dating back to Anita Hill, went digging she didn't find anything that made him "look worse than even that"?

